<Layout title="Aggregate technology · HQD" active="technology"><section class="container page-heading"><p class="breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span>Technology</p><p class="eyebrow">UNDERSTAND THE CONNECTION</p><h1>Precision.<br />In another direction<span>.</span></h1><p>An aggregate extends the spindle’s working direction through a mechanical transmission and a different tool orientation.</p></section><section class="container technology-page"><img src={products[1].image} alt="HSK63F adjustable aggregate" width="800" height="800"/><div><h2>From spindle rotation<br />to tool access.</h2><p>A machine spindle drives the aggregate input. The internal transmission transfers rotation to the output tool, which can be oriented away from the main spindle axis.</p><h2>The interface comes first.</h2><p>The HQD range offers ISO30 and HSK63F inputs with ER25 output tooling. Match the input interface, torque restraint and physical envelope to your machine before selecting the output configuration.</p><a href="/products/" class="text-link">FIND YOUR CONNECTION <span>↗</span></a></div></section><section class="container content-grid"><article class="content-tile"><p class="eyebrow">ANGLE & CONFIGURATION</p><h2>Adjustable, fixed or multi-output.</h2><p>Adjustable heads change the output orientation. Fixed-angle heads provide a dedicated geometry. Dual- and four-output heads combine multiple tool positions. These configurations expand access; they do not turn a three-axis machine into a simultaneous five-axis machine.</p></article><article class="content-tile"><p class="eyebrow">SPEED & TOOLING</p><h2>Read both sides of the ratio.</h2><p>Input speed and output speed are different on a speed-increasing head. Follow the model-specific technical table. Saw blades can have lower limits than drilling tools. The approved tool, mounting and application determine the final operating settings.</p></article></section></Layout>
